Subject: [PATCH] Blu-ray: draw UHD disc menus as the HDR graphics they are

A UHD Blu-ray authors its graphics in the dynamic range of the video they
accompany. Over HDR10 or Dolby Vision video, BD-J images and HDMV palettes
hold BT.2020 PQ code values. The Call of the Wild names every menu image
*_BT2020_HDR and Avatar: Fire and Ash *_bt2020_hdr, and neither disc
carries SDR art. Kodi decoded these as BT.709 sRGB and sent them through
the GUI composite at 203 nit reference white, so a menu authored at about
283 nits (code 157/255) showed at 68 nits, and its BT.2020 colours were
treated as BT.709 and washed out.

Flag the disc's menu graphics as PQ (m_isHDROverlay) while the play item
being played carries HDR video (its primary video stream's libbluray
dynamic_range_type). HDMV palettes then use the BT.2020 limited-range
matrix. A BD-J page drawn before the play item that says what it is is
flagged again on the next flush, or sent again when the play item changes.

Menus then take the route HDR PGS already takes:
- Kodi's HDR output draws them straight into the PQ back buffer.
- CB1's Dolby Vision circuits draw them into the PQ subtitle layer, which
  each circuit composes. They no longer depend on the SDR GUI being
  composed.
- SDR output converts them to sRGB.
BD-J pixels are straight-alpha ARGB rather than a palette. They get the
same limited-range and sRGB conversions as a PQ palette, with each
distinct colour converted once.

Over SDR video nothing changes.

